WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.110
Speaker 1: I want to start today with something
that many of us at this point intuitively

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:10.099
probably understand and something that's also
been made explicit by the FBI and other law

00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:15.519
enforcement agencies over the last year or
two, which is that at this point in time for

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:21.060
the United States, without saying anything
about time's past necessarily right now, the

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:27.820
U.S. is facing as way more domestic terror
threats, as foreign terror threats.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:34.690
There's a very interesting Newsweek article
about this which says the U.S. is facing far

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:40.100
more domestic terror threats than those from
international parties, according to the FBI.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:45.350
Timothy Langan, assistant director of the
bureau's counterterrorism division, told lawmakers

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:51.550
Wednesday that the U.S. is approaching more
than 2700 domestic terrorism threats.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:55.420
The number of international terror threats
is under 1000.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:01:01.359
Langen also gave statistics on terrorism related
deaths in the U.S. from 2015 to 2020.

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:07.710
There were 80 reported international terrorism
related deaths, compared with 83 deaths linked

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.000
to domestic terrorism.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.729
Langan's testimony follows FBI Director Christopher
Wray statements to Congress earlier this month

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:20.990
when he said the number of domestic terrorism
cases has exploded in the last year and a

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:21.990
half.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:26.229
A recent AP poll found more Americans are
concerned about threats from extremist groups

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:31.850
based on based in the U.S. as opposed to those
from other countries.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:37.590
Sixty five percent of U.S. adults report being
extremely or very concerned about domestic

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:45.229
group threats, compared with 50 percent feeling
the same about foreign extremist groups.

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:47.690
We there's a couple things here.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:57.850
One is it's really common that people's fear
of things is often out of line with the actual

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.729
danger of that thing.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:03.399
We see this with vaccine stuff, right?

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:06.869
There are lots of people who are afraid of
covid, but this movement of people afraid

00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:10.690
of the vaccine when really it's covid you
should be much more afraid of.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.290
That would be one example.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:18.710
In general, people tend to worry much more
about, for example, being the victims of a

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:23.490
terrorist attack, despite it being an insanely
unlikely that any of us would ever be.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:27.960
Doesn't mean some people won't be, but statistically
speaking, insanely unlikely versus things

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:33.690
we don't think much about, like getting in
a car every day, which is actually a comparatively

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:38.060
dangerous thing given the number of people
who die yearly from terrorism in the US versus

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:43.380
the number who die in car accidents or being
more afraid of flying than driving in a car,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:47.400
even though the driving in a car is far more
dangerous.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:53.280
OK, so so that's one thing that oftentimes,
even though the domestic terror threat is

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.340
a bigger threat, people think of the idea
of often it's dangerous Muslims from the Middle

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.800
East or sometimes it would be brown immigrants
from Latin America or whatever the trope of

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.410
the week, month or year happens to be.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:15.980
But then, number two, you know, there are
entire media apparatuses in the United States

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:24.020
that are very similar to what we see in other
countries, radicalize people into what is

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:33.810
often referred to in the U.S. as a jihadist
terror or Islamic or Islamist terror.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:39.220
We have the equivalent in the United States
of these media outlets that spread hate and

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.810
so fear and radicalize people.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:47.000
And many Americans don't recognize them as
being the sort of domestic equivalents of

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.370
the stuff that we say, well, how did these
people, these people end up getting radicalized

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.830
in the ISIS, for example?

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:56.080
And you look at the media and the Internet
content, but then we wonder, how did so many

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.590
people end up getting radicalized to the point
where they rioted on January 6th or they put

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:05.760
pipe bombs in Washington, D.C. or whatever
the case may be?

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.950
And then you look at what we have happening
on Facebook and on Newsmax to some degree,

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:16.060
a Fox News and Alex Jones and so many others.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:19.270
This stuff radicalizes people as well.

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:26.800
And it should come as no surprise that when
the rubber hits the road, the domestic terror

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.790
is actually far more of a threat mathematically
than the foreign terror.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:35.880
And yet another report from the FBI confirms
exactly that.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:44.140
Now, if you think that although the number
may be greater, it makes sense because, well,

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.920
you're in the U.S., so the Americans are already
here.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:51.270
That doesn't mean that they are more dangerous.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:52.440
On average.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:53.490
There may be more of them.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:57.590
And, you know, if it's eighty three versus
eighty deaths from terror, the fact that there

00:04:57.590 --> 00:04:59.220
are more threats doesn't mean.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:04.470
That much, I would disagree, the fact that
there are that many potential nodes of radicalism

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:10.950
and terrorism domestically means that there's
way more people who could be activated, could

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.770
be activated by the exact type of rhetoric
that we've been fighting against.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:15.710
Let me know your thoughts

